"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8935],{88935:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"sankcii-ssha-platezhnaya-set-a7","locale":"en","title":"US proposes barring bank transfers through hundreds of A7 payment-network companies","description":"On October 1, the US Treasury designated A7 a criminal organization and proposed prohibiting bank transfers through its sub-agents. How this affects imports.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"On October 1, 2026, the US Treasury designated Russia\'s A7 payment network a significant transnational criminal organization. Its financial-crime unit, FinCEN, simultaneously proposed prohibiting American banks from making any transfers involving A7 sub-agents. These are overseas front companies through which the network processes Russian clients\' payments."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"The A7 payment network was launched in 2024 by Moldovan businessman Ilan Shor and Promsvyazbank. It charges 0.3% per transfer and issues A7A5, a digital stablecoin pegged to the ruble. The Russian part of A7 and its stablecoin have been under US sanctions since August 2025, but payments continued through foreign firms. US authorities also link the network to Iran, alleging use by the Central Bank of Iran and the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps. FinCEN\'s proposed prohibition relies on legislation against Russian money laundering."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"A7 operates at considerable scale. It has hundreds of sub-agents in the UAE, Hong Kong, Turkey, Kyrgyzstan and other countries, holding accounts at about 435 banks in 83 countries. From January 2025 to June 2026, they processed more than $17 billion in dollar payments. That is only part of turnover: by January 2026 the network claimed cumulative transfers of 7.5 trillion rubles. Promsvyazbank estimated that A7 handled almost 12% of Russia\'s foreign trade in the first half of 2025. FinCEN gives a similar figure of almost 13% for all of 2025."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Why can the US stop other countries\' payments? A dollar transfer between banks in different countries almost always passes through an account at an American bank. It resembles a parcel going through a sorting center: if the center refuses it, it will not arrive even when sender and recipient live elsewhere. Such an account is called a correspondent account. The prohibition cuts A7 sub-agents off from that center. But the impact may extend beyond dollars: foreign banks holding sub-agents\' accounts risk sanctions themselves and may simply close those accounts in every currency. Banks will privately receive an expanding list of the firms involved."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"Sanctions on A7 do not directly affect an ordinary person\'s wallet because the network serves companies. The indirect channel is import prices. Each new settlement obstacle makes international payment slower and more expensive, and businesses include these costs in goods prices. The size of any increase cannot be estimated. A7 was already sanctioned by the US in 2025 and continued operating through new firms."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"The FinCEN rule is still a proposal, with a 30-day comment period after publication. The US Treasury expects other countries and banks to follow, but whether they will is unknown. Buyers of imported electronics and spare parts should not treat this as a reason to stockpile: the effect will unfold over months and mix with ruble exchange-rate changes. Yet payment-network restrictions are not irrelevant, because expensive settlement ultimately enters goods prices."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"What is the A7 payment network?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"An international transfer system launched in 2024 by Ilan Shor and Promsvyazbank. It processes Russian companies\' payments through overseas front firms, charges 0.3% and issues the ruble stablecoin A7A5."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"What US sanctions targeted A7 in October 2026?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"On October 1, 2026, the US Treasury designated A7 a transnational criminal organization. FinCEN proposed barring American banks from transfers through hundreds of its sub-agents, which hold accounts at about 435 banks in 83 countries."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"Will sanctions on A7 make imports more expensive?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"Possibly, but the extent is unknown. A7 handled almost 12–13% of Russia\'s foreign trade, and every new obstacle makes settlement slower and more expensive. Businesses incorporate those costs into goods prices."}],"sources":[{"label":"US sanctions the A7 payment system","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/8991793"},{"label":"US prepares to prohibit payments through hundreds of foreign A7 companies","url":"https://thebell.io/ssha-gotovyat-zapret-na-platezhi-cherez-sotni-zarubezhnykh-kompaniy-a7-po-otsenke-psb-cherez-etu-set-shlo-pochti-12-vneshney-torgovli-rossii"},{"label":"Proposal of Special Measure Regarding A7 Network Sub-Agents","url":"https://www.fincen.gov/system/files/2026-10/9714-A7-NETWORK-NPRM.pdf"}],"sourceBodySha256":"e928692ceb98a757973abed5533e72f30df7536893758054b08fc807c779360a"}')}}]);